ing and now only now do  we play g4 it's very important to play  knight c e4 before you play g4 if you  play g4 first white can get in a lot of  trouble for example if you play g4 here  on move 11 g4 queen g6 now if you try  knight ce4 they're simply bishop b6  white's king will never be safe and  black has no problems here but after g4  the queen must move again queen also  must stay tied to the bishop so she's  really only got two spots e5 or d5 now  if you've never seen the position before  you might think that e5 is a little suss  because you're lined up with this rook  here but in fact queen to e5 is the best  move and queen to d5 loses the game  straight away after f takes g7 rook hg8  knight f6 it's a fork queen back to d6  knight ge4 now hitting the queen again  queen e7 knight takes g8 rook takes g8  and bishop g5 the queen cannot stay tied  to this bishop f6 is a good try so that  after bishop takes f6 now you say hey  you can't take my bishop because your  bishop will hang well after queen f7  knight takes c5 black realizes that  their bishop is also hanging queen takes  f6 knight takes e6 white is up a full  rook and will be able to safeguard their  king white wins this one so queen d5 is  not playable here black has to play  queen e5 so after the queen moves to e5  we're gonna hit the queen again with  knight to f3 queen d5 we're going to  take this pawn on g7 now hitting the  rook rook hg8 knight f6 getting the fork  on the queen in the rook and after queen  d6 we actually don't take the rook you  might ask yourself in this position why  not just take the rook and be up the  exchange well black's attack is  unfortunately just too strong if you  play knight takes g8 here white's  trading one of their best pieces and  black just gets the free reload with  rook takes g8 white's king safety center  and development are all super bad  white's king is never going to be safe  for the rest of the game they don't have  enough compensation for the exchange and  b